==Old Saxon==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|osx|gem-pro|*wurmiz}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|osx}} {{m|gem-pro|*wr̥mis}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|wirm}}, {{cog|ang|wyrm}}, {{cog|goh|wurm}} ({{cog|de|Wurm}}), {{cog|non|ormr}} ({{cog|sv|orm||serpent}}), {{cog|got|𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐌼𐍃||worm, serpent}}, {{cog|nl|worm}} and {{m|nl|wurm}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of {{cog|la|vermis||worm}}, {{cog|lt|varmas||midge}}, {{cog|orv|вермие||locusts, worms}}, {{cog|grc|ῥόμος||earthworm}} (originally *{{m|grc||ϝράμος}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/wurm/|lang=osx}}

